\begin{abstract}
This report presents the design and preliminary development of NaviGoat, an autonomous, socially aware robotic guide for visually impaired individuals. Built on a Pioneer 3-AT mobile base, the system combines 3D LiDAR-based SLAM, Nav2 path planning, Model Predictive Control (MPC) and RGB-D pedestrian tracking to enable safe navigation in dynamic environments. A guiding pole and three-level haptic and audio feedback system aim to provide intuitive guidance while minimising cognitive workload. Preliminary work demonstrates robot teleoperation, simulated mapping, pedestrian tracking, object recognition and speech-based interaction. The proposed system will be evaluated through controlled navigation experiments assessing collision avoidance, motion smoothness, feedback latency and user experience.
\end{abstract}


\section{\textbf{Introduction and Research Hypothesis}}
% Introduction: what robotic system are you designing and what is its purpose? What is the hypothesis (-es) that you will be testing regarding its performance, and associated human factors?

Navigating unfamiliar and crowded environments remains a significant challenge for visually impaired individuals. Although options such as white canes and guide dogs provide valuable assistance, they cannot provide detailed information about the surrounding environment or autonomously plan unknown routes.
<
The dynamic nature of social environments, which are prone to moving obstacles and unpredictable pedestrians, creates further complications. These complex situations should be tackled, while ensuring the user's comfort and the respect of social norms. These observations motivate the research question:\\

%\textbf{\emph{How can a socially aware guide robot with intelligent feedback enable visually impaired individuals to navigate dynamic environments?}}\\

\textbf{\emph{Can a socially aware guide robot with intelligent feedback enable smoother and safer navigation for visually impaired users across dynamic environments while ensuring a positive experience?}}\\

This report proposes Pete, an autonomous guide robot built on a Pioneer 3-AT mobile base. The system uses LiDAR-based Simultaneous Localisation and Mapping (SLAM) to map its surroundings, estimate its position and plan socially compliant paths. The user follows the robot through a guiding handle, while a three level haptic and audio feedback system communicates navigation cues and warnings according to their urgency.

\section{\textbf{Literature Review}}
% Do not use only blogs/web references for this, or just GenAI resources, but explore scientific bibliographic resources (IEEExplore, ACM, Google Scholar, Arxiv, DBLP, …) to demonstrate knowledge of the related work.
%Note: Could merge with another section if need be 

% Primary and secondary research was conducted to make informed design choices for NaviGoat. For primary research, contact was made with the Royal National Institute of Blind People \cite{rnib2026} to obtain first hand accounts and assess what features would be more useful and valued in the robot implementation. We are awaiting a response on whether it is possible to conduct a short survey, which would allow better requirements capture.


Our research focused on identifying and reviewing existing assistive navigation systems, recognizing their limitations in the context of our research focus. 

For their outdoor and uneven terrain navigation   and , quadruped robotsWang et al. \cite{wang2023quadruped} evaluated quadruped and wheeled robotic guides for visually impaired users, concluding that walking noise and unstable movements of quadruped robots reduced user comfort and trust. On the basis of these findings, a wheeled base morphology was selected, with the aim of achieving smooth navigation. 


Furthermore, Guerrerio et al. \cite{guerreiro2019cabot} developed CaBot, an autonomous guide robot with a vibrating handle for user guidance, demonstrating the feasibility of an approach similar to NaviGoat's. Nevertheless, their evaluation did not account for dynamic environments with moving pedestrians, which is an area requiring further investigation as the authors identified. This motivates our focus on socially aware navigation. Singamaneni et al. \cite{singamaneni2024social} emphasized the importance of pedestrian movements and keeping safe distance. This motivated NaviGoat's use of Model Predictive Control (MPC) for planning safe paths around moving pedestrians.


Additionally, effective communication with the user is fundamental. Martinez et al. \cite{martinez2014feedback} explored audio and haptic feedback for visually impaired navigation, concluding that audio guidance leads to higher cognitive workload when it comes to experienced white cane users during short-range navigation. Building on this, our project proposes a three level feedback system, using directional handle vibrations for routine navigation, wristband vibrations for cautionary alerts, and audio feedback for urgent warnings. 


Overall, these findings shaped this project's design decisions, which includes a wheeled robotic base, socially aware navigation, and an urgency based multimodal feedback, in order to provide safe and comfortable guidance to the user.

\section{\textbf{Proposed System Design}}
%Outline of your design, explaining design choices, and justification for your selected design.


\subsection{\textbf{System Overview}}

NaviGoat is built on a Pioneer 3-AT mobile base, equipped with a Unitree L1 3D LiDAR and an Orbbec Femto Bolt RGB-D camera for environmental perception, localisation and autonomous navigation. A guiding pole connects the robot to the user, providing both physical guidance and an interface for user interaction.

The guiding pole features two degrees of freedom, with passive gas springs connecting it to the robot's base. These actuators provide directional resistance to guide the user's movements, while sensors around the handle detect whether the user is holding the pole.

To communicate navigation information without overwhelming the user, a multimodal feedback system is proposed, organised into three levels of urgency:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item {Handle vibrations:} Routine navigation cues, including directional guidance and upcoming turns.
    \item {Vibrating wristbands:} Cautionary alerts, such as the direction of approaching pedestrians or nearby hazards.
    \item {Audio feedback through an earpiece:} Urgent warnings, including imminent collisions and emergency stops.
\end{enumerate}

This feedback hierarchy aims to minimise unnecessary interruptions while ensuring that critical information reaches the user promptly. Together, the guiding pole, wearable feedback devices and autonomous navigation system are intended to provide an intuitive and accessible guiding experience.

\subsection{\textbf{Hardware Design}}

\subsubsection{\textbf{Morphology}}
A wheeled base was chosen over a quadrupedal base to prioritise smooth movement and user comfort. The walking gait of a quadrupedal robot can introduce vibrations that may be transmitted through the guiding pole, reducing user comfort. Furthermore the noise and ground vibrations generated as they move could cause discomfort or intimidation to nearby pedestrians and increasing the user's sense of isolation.

Particular attention has been paid to chose the morphology such that the robot is not intimidating is the social acceptance of the robot, as users may be reluctant to use a device that attracts unwanted attention or makes them feel uncomfortable in public. To address this, NaviGoat adopts a friendly, goat-inspired design, aiming to evoke the positive perception commonly associated with guide dogs. Additionally, external lighting and a projection system will communicate the robot's intentions to nearby pedestrians, facilitating interactions and improving predictability in shared spaces.


\subsubsection{\textbf{Guide Pole}}

The guide pole is the users main physical link to the robot and the primary method of feedback.  

Its morphology was specifically chosen to resemble a white cane to allow users to quickly adapt to a familiar shape.  

The pole is mounted onto the base through a Cardam universal gimbal joint, constrained by four passive pistons mounted on aluminium extrusion around it, which allows movement of the pole with 2DoF (pitch and roll). 

An IMU connected at the base of the pole measures its angle and acceleration. This lets the user indicate a preferred direction by pushing the pole. It also further provides the pole-acceleration which is used as a measure of smoothness section~\ref{Exp_Validation}.

