\subsubsection{\textbf{Simultaneous Localization and Mapping}}
A LiDAR was chosen over a depth camera for SLAM due to its greater robustness to varying lighting conditions, particularly direct sunlight and low-light environments, which the robot is expected to encounter during outdoor navigation. LiDARs also have greater range than depth cameras which will be beneficial since we will be operating outside.
Data from a 3D LiDAR, an IMU and the wheel encoders on the P3-AT base will be fused to improve localisation accuracy. The LiDAR will provide environmental geometry while the IMU and encoders will provide motion estimates to assist with pose estimation \cite{s26103045}. Moving objects including people need to be filtered out from the map. 3D SLAM was chosen over 2D SLAM since the robot must be able to operate in crowded spaces. In such environments many points will hit people instead of walls, which would make mapping the environment difficult. The system will generate 3D maps of the environment which can also be saved and reused for future navigation. Slices of the 3D point-cloud can be used to generate 2D occupancy grids for navigation. 

\subsubsection{\textbf{Autonomy and Navigation}}

The autonomous navigation system is designed with a focus on user experience, safety and practical use. Beyond providing a collision-free route, the robot should ideally guide the user safely, maintain comfortable motion, and assist complementary subsystems in enabling the user to navigate various social situations naturally.

The navigation system uses the ROS 2 Navigation Stack (Nav2) for global path planning, combined with a custom Model Predictive Control (MPC) controller for local navigation. Nav2 will use the 2D occupancy grid generated from the SLAM system to plan a route to the destination. A local costmap, updated using live LiDAR measurements, will represent nearby obstacles and changes in the environment.

A custom MPC controller will be integrated into the Nav2 Controller Server. MPC was selected for its ability to anticipate the robot's motion and explicitly account for kinematic and safety constraints. At each control step, it will optimise a trajectory over a finite prediction horizon, balancing progress towards the destination, obstacle clearance and smoothness of motion. Only the first control command will be executed before replanning using updated sensor measurements.

The resulting linear and angular velocity commands will be transmitted to the Pioneer 3-AT through the \texttt{/cmd\_vel} topic. Nav2 will retain responsibility for global planning and navigation recovery, while the custom controller will provide the flexibility required for the socially aware behaviours described below.

\paragraph{\textbf{Social Navigation}}

The primary challenge is guiding visually impaired users through crowds without causing collisions or uncomfortable interactions with pedestrians. This requires understanding where pedestrians are, predicting their movements and choosing trajectories that respect social conventions.

A forward-facing RGB-D camera will be used for pedestrian detection. YOLO will identify pedestrians, and depth measurements will be transformed into the map reference frame.

To improve tracking robustness, pedestrian detections will be associated with the corresponding clusters in the 3D LiDAR point cloud. This will allow LiDAR measurements belonging to pedestrians to be distinguished from the static environment.

Pedestrian detections will be associated across successive observations to estimate individual positions and velocities. A tracking algorithm, based on Kalman filtering, will maintain these estimates and predict pedestrian positions over the MPC planning horizon \cite{alahi2016social}.

These predictions will be incorporated into the MPC objective function to minimise collision risk. Additional costs will discourage behaviours such as cutting through groups, passing unnecessarily close to people or making abrupt manoeuvres.
As a stretch goal, we will investigate Deep Residual Model Predictive Control (DR-MPC), proposed by Han et al. \cite{han2025drmpc}. This approach combines an MPC baseline with residual reinforcement learning to improve social navigation through experience.

\paragraph{\textbf{Person Following}}

Person following will reuse shared YOLO-World pedestrian detections and RGB-D position estimates to follow a designated individual. A target-selection module will associate the chosen person with a tracked pedestrian, optionally initialised through contact recognition. The target's position will be used to generate updated Nav2 goals at a specified following distance, while the navigation controller handles obstacle avoidance. Target identity will be monitored across frames, with loss of tracking triggering a safe pause rather than switching to another pedestrian.

% \paragraph{Object Tracking}

% The object detection system described previously will also support object-directed navigation. Detected objects will be localised using depth measurements and transformed into map coordinates through TF2. Their positions can then be used to generate navigation goals, allowing the robot to guide the user towards a requested object rather than simply reporting its location.

\paragraph{\textbf{GPS-Based Outdoor Navigation}}

To extend navigation beyond pre-mapped areas, a GPS navigation layer will guide the user through unfamiliar outdoor environments. Nav2 can only plan within the map built by SLAM, this layer provides the long-range route and supplies Nav2 with intermediate goals along it. Routes will be planned on OpenStreetMap (OSM) which also include accessibility tags such as signalled crossings and tactile paving, allowing a custom route cost to favour safer crossings over shorter routes. Since GPS errors of 5–30 m are common in urban areas, a localisation module will fuse GPS fixes with IMU and wheel-encoder data. A route follower will select goal points a short distance ahead along the route, which tolerates GPS noise and allows the robot to rejoin the route after avoiding obstacles. A mission manager will monitor progress, replan when the robot leaves the route and pause at road crossings.

