Fig.~\ref{fig:speech} shows how a request is processed. The microphone is continuously monitored and the audio is segmented into phrases by a voice activity detector. Every phrase gets transcribed on the Jetson by Whisper \cite{radford2023whisper}, an open-source speech recognition model based on an encoder–decoder transformer. The small English-only base model runs on the GPU, and is about four to five times faster than on the CPU, allowing for low enough delay for conversation. A phrase is only acted on when it includes the wake word "Pete", the robot's name.  Stop and help requests are interpreted by fast rules, getting an immediate response. The other requests are read by a small open-source vision-language model (Qwen3-VL 2B~\cite{bai2025qwen3vl}) that also handles scene description. According to the request, the controller selects an operating mode (e.g. Follow, Navigation, GPS, Perception) and sends the relevant data to the corresponding module. The robot speaks only to answer perception questions and to give urgent safety warnings, because audio guidance can increase cognitive workload \cite{martinez2014feedback}. Urgent safety warnings interrupt answer in progress. 

\subsubsection{\textbf{Object Finding and Scene Description}}
The user will be able to ask the robot to find everyday objects, such as cups, chairs or door handles, or to describe what is in front of them (Fig.~\ref{fig:object_finding}). Requests arrive as text from the speech interface (Section~\ref{sec:speech}). Those the command parser does not recognise, and scene descriptions, are handled by Qwen3-VL, similar to the approach of Lin et al.~\cite{s26103045}, while an open vocabulary YOLO-World model \cite{cheng2024yoloworld} runs continuously on the RGB-D camera feed. Both models will run locally on the Jetson, so the system works without an internet connection and with low latency. Qwen3-VL 2B was chosen as it uses around 2\,GB of memory, leaving room on the 8\,GB Jetson for the other subsystems. The same YOLO-World model will also be used for pedestrian detection, and objects the robot can guide the user to will be published in the same ROS~2 package as the detected people, so they can be used directly as navigation goals. Object positions will be given relative to the user as a direction and a distance taken from the camera's depth image.


