4 & Static obstacles + $\geq$10 pedestrians (in groups and walking alone) &
Realistic crowd test of social navigation and pedestrian tracking \\

5 & Designated person walking a route with turns, other pedestrians and temporary occlusions &
Person following: distance error, target retention, incorrect target switches and safe stopping on target loss \\

\hline
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

To evaluate the success of each run, six quantifiable criteria are chosen, representing safety and smoothness: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Collision: Any contact between the robot/user and a pedestrian/obstacle seen via video
    \item Near Misses: minimum distance between robot/user $<$ 1.5m 
    \item Abrupt Stops: decelerations above 0.5\,m/s$^2$, taken from the filtered odometry.
    \item Smoothness: acceleration measured on the base of the pole with an IMU 
    \item Time to complete run 
    \item Time to communicate feedback at each urgency level 
\end{enumerate}

To evaluate user experience and cognitive load the NASA Task Load Index (TLX) questionnaire will be used by obtaining an overload workload score based on mental, physical and temporal demand,  performance, effort, and frustration \cite{nasa_tlx_web}.

\section{\textbf{Preliminary Work and Results}}

\subsection{\textbf{Manual Movement of Base}}
The Pioneer 3-AT has been brought up under ROS~2 Jazzy using the \texttt{p2os2} driver, connected to the base's controller over USB-serial.
The robot has been driven by both by keyboard teleoperation and by a scripted test node that commands a set speed for a set time. The wheel odometry and battery voltage can be obtained reliably. For safety during this initial testing, speed was capped at 0.3m/s.

\subsection{\textbf{Hardware}}
A pole has been obtained and preliminarily fastened to the P3-AT. A first CAD design of the grip has been completed.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{images/mishSLOP.png}
    \caption{Sketch of First Iteration of the Handle Grip with Vibrational Motors}
    \label{fig:placeholder}
\end{figure}



\subsection{\textbf{Electronics}}
The wristband electronics architecture was developed and components
selected to minimise bulk and improve comfort. The XIAO ESP32-C3
was chosen for its compact size, integrated Wi-Fi and Arduino
support, alongside thin 10\,mm ERM motors, two DRV8833 drivers
for independent motor control and an LSM6DSOX IMU for orientation
tracking. 

\subsection{\textbf{Software}}
\paragraph{\textbf{SLAM}}
Using the Unitree L1 LiDAR and its internal IMU with RTAB-Map can localise the robot to within 5cm of its actual position running on the Jetson. Before mapping the point cloud is filtered using a three-scan window, accounting for robot movement to identify and reduce points associated with moving objects.


\paragraph{\textbf{Navigation}}

\subsection{Preliminary Work}

The Pioneer 3-AT simulation was adapted to support multiple Gazebo environments through parameterised ROS 2 launch files, allowing the same navigation setup to be evaluated in indoor and warehouse scenarios. The URDF/Xacro configuration was adjusted to integrate the 3D LiDAR and maintain consistent odometry and TF frames.

For the 2D occupancy map, RTAB-Map's ground-height filtering was used to prevent floor returns and low-lying, traversable features from being marked as obstacles. Two-dimensional ray tracing was enabled to infer free space along LiDAR rays, reducing unknown cells in the resulting map. This configuration follows the approach described by RTAB-Map developer Mathieu Labb\'e \cite{labbe_rtabmap_projection}. Figure~\ref{fig:ground_filtering} compares the resulting maps before and after filtering in both environments.

