\paragraph{\textbf{Social navigation}}
The Orbbec Femto Bolt camera was integrated with a YOLO-World model to detect and track people.
Detected people were identified using bounding boxes, with depth measurements extracted from the central region of each bounding box. These depth measurements, combined with the camera's intrinsic parameters, were used to estimate each person's position in 2D space.
Successive position measurements were processed using a Kalman filter to estimate each person's velocity and predict their short-term future position. The system was successfully tested on the Jetson, achieving a preliminary latency of approximately 150 ms.

\paragraph{\textbf{Person Following}}

The existing YOLO-World and RGB-D pedestrian tracking prototype provides the perception foundation for person following. Preliminary design has established a target-selection and following architecture that reuses shared pedestrian position estimates and communicates dynamic goals to Nav2. Target-loss handling and identity continuity have been identified as key requirements, particularly when pedestrians cross or become occluded. Closed-loop person-following tests remain future work.


\paragraph{\textbf{Contact Recognition}}

Initial development has focused on implementing a modular facial recognition pipeline using pretrained SCRFD and ArcFace models. A local prototype codebase has been established, with components for face detection, facial alignment, embedding extraction and cosine similarity-based identity matching. The system architecture also incorporates contact enrolment, confidence thresholding and multi-frame verification to reduce incorrect identifications. An interface has been specified for passing recognised identities, bounding boxes and timestamps to the person-following subsystem. Deployment on the Jetson Orin Nano, integration with the Orbbec Femto Bolt and quantitative performance evaluation remain the next development stages.

\paragraph{\textbf{Object Finding and Scene Description}}
A working prototype was run entirely on the Jetson with the colour and depth streams of the Orbbec Femto Bolt (Fig.~\ref{fig:object_results}). Indirect requests were mapped onto known objects, so ``I would like a place to sit down'' located a chair (Fig.~\ref{fig:chair}), and the user was told when none was in view (Fig.~\ref{fig:no_chair}). Distances were measured as the median depth over the centre of each detected object, and directions from the camera's calibrated field of view. In scene descriptions, the number of people and the object positions were added from the detector rather than the language model, which otherwise invented them (Fig.~\ref{fig:lib}). Outdoor terrains were simulated by placing the camera in front of photographs (Fig.~\ref{fig:park}), so distances in those tests refer to the photograph. On the Jetson, replies took on average 2\,s.

