\paragraph{\textbf{Robotic Dog}}
Replacing the wheeled base with a quadrupedal base to overcome its current terrain limitations, allowing users to be guided up and down stairs and uneven terrains. 

\paragraph{\textbf{Smart Glasses}}
Smart glasses would provide an elegant solution by incorporating the earpiece, microphone and speaker functionality, as well as providing a non-intrusive camera that is mounted to the head so the robot can know in which direction the person is facing.

\paragraph{\textbf{Emergency Response}}
The system could detect if the user has fallen over and contact emergency services and share their current location if they fail to get back up.

\paragraph{\textbf{Object Interaction Assistance}}
A potential future extension to the wristband will guide the user’s hand towards objects such as door handles, card readers and pedestrian-crossing buttons. The rover’s RGB-D camera will locate the target in the rover’s reference frame. A wrist-mounted UWB tag will exchange ranging signals with four anchors distributed across the rover’s width, length and height, allowing the controller to estimate the wrist’s XYZ position.

% The difference between the estimated hand and target positions will determine the required movement. The four motors will indicate left, right, up and down, with distinct pulse patterns for forward and backward movement. IMU-based orientation compensation will adjust the motor mapping as the wrist rotates. This mode will be explicitly activated to distinguish movement instructions from hazard alerts. Positioning accuracy, including the offset between the wrist and fingers, will require experimental validation.

%=============short version==================
%A potential extension would guide the user’s hand towards door handles, card readers or pedestrian-crossing buttons. The rover’s RGB-D camera would locate the target, while a wrist-mounted UWB tag and four anchors distributed across the rover’s width, length and height would estimate wrist XYZ in the same reference frame. The position difference would determine corrective cues: four motors for left, right, up and down, with distinct pulse patterns for forward and backward movement. IMU-based compensation would adjust the mapping as the wrist rotates. Explicit mode activation would distinguish guidance from hazard alerts. Positioning accuracy and the wrist-to-finger offset would require experimental validation.

\paragraph{\textbf{Collaborative Mapping}}
As the robots navigate their environments the maps they generate could be uploaded to a shared database and accessed by other robots. This would reduce the need to repeatedly map the same environments while also allowing newly detected changes to be shared across the fleet.

\paragraph{\textbf{Proactive Hazard Awareness}}
The LiDAR detects obstacles but not what they are. The vision language model could warn the user about hazards it would miss, such as a door opening into their path, a wet floor sign or a person holding a knife, passing them into the feedback hierarchy according to their urgency.


\paragraph{\textbf{Microphone Mute Button}}
Following testing of Whisper, a microphone mute button
could be added to the guide handle to prevent unintended
activation when background speech is recognised as the
wake phrase ``Hey Pete''.
% \paragraph{\textbf{Object Memory and Personal Objects}}
% The robot could remember where a small set of personal items, such as house keys or a wallet, were last seen by storing their positions on the SLAM map. The user could then ask ``Hey Pete, where are my keys?'' even when they are out of view. Items would be enrolled by holding them up to the camera and saying ``Hey Pete, these are my keys'', so the user's own items can be told apart from similar ones. As an item may be moved without the robot seeing it, the robot would say when it was last seen, e.g.\ ``Your keys were on the table two hours ago''.

